[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'One of the questions you guys ask me most often is what file manager I recommend. You have a photo editor you\'re happy with, perhaps Photo Lab, Nitro, or Darktable. And'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'now you want to pick up a decent app to manage the photos you edit in it. And this is one of those tricky questions'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that is very difficult to answer because the final decision depends on what platform you have— Windows, Linux, or Mac, how much you want to spend, whether you are against subscriptions, what features you are looking for, whether you need'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'AI tools, etc. In the past, I might have recommended Adobe Bridge, digiKam, Peakto, Pixel Path, ACDSee, Photo Mechanic, XnView, or iMatch—'

[00:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'depending on who asks. And now I have a new option to add to that list. Today I\'m reviewing the'

[01:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'F-Lap. It\'s a free, open-source, cross-platform file manager with smart AI, a solid feature set, and a thoughtful design that belies its'

[01:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'young age, but as far as I can see, it has one potentially critical flaw. Welcome to the channel,'

[01:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'friends. My name is Andy Hutchinson. I\'m a typical British- Australian tech journalist from the past and a long-time camera and photography enthusiast with a weakness for'

[01:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'camera bags. Like all of my videos, this one has no sponsors or affiliate programs and is only made possible by my patient wife and these incredible'

[01:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'legends in my Patreon and Substack communities. Link in the description if you want to support independent content without ads, with lots of sarcasm, a few strong words,'

[02:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and a minimum of artificial enthusiasm. I have to admit that in recent months I have become somewhat weary of the vast number of hastily written applications that are sent to me for review. Not because'

[02:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'they necessarily have terrible interfaces, but because they all have a strangely similar look and feel. So it was a real pleasure to use an app that looks like it was actually'

[02:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'designed by a human. The app has one of the most polished interfaces I\'ve used in a long time, and I love the font choice, its size, color scheme, and use of icons. It all looks very'

[02:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'stylish, and I honestly have a hard time believing that this is a free app . We have a fairly traditional layout here. We are in masonry mode now. We are now in the standard library view'

[03:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: ', which can be filtered however you like , and on the side are the main modes, which we will now talk about. Also, we have a toolbar here. For example, we can view the metadata of any'

