AUDIO: 'uses visual intelligence technologies. For example, if I search for "fair " or "horses," we get very accurate and fast search results. Of course, it doesn\'t have to be just individual'

[05:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'words. You can type something like "people on the beach" and you\'ll get similar search results. Next we have tags. I have n\'t added any to my photos yet. We have a calendar view mode'

[05:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: ', so we can break down photos by years, months, and days. We have search by location and can also filter by camera. So, I can just show all the photos I'

[06:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'took on my XT4. Double-tapping any photo opens it, and as you can see, there are controls at the top for adding tags, comments, rotating the image, and more.'

[06:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'All the best apps are created by developers who understand their main goal and don\'t stray too far from it. This is definitely true for Lr. You get'

[06:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'file management, a library, smart albums and collections, and some great AI-powered features that run right on the device. I installed Lr without any expectations and was genuinely'

[06:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'surprised by how mature it turned out to be. So let\'s first discuss all the positive points before moving on to that " stumbling block" I mentioned at the beginning. It has some'

[06:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'editing features, but they are pretty basic. Just a few sliders and presets. This is not a criticism, as it is obvious that they are only needed so that you can temporarily brighten an underexposed'

[07:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'shot to better understand what to do with it, not for professional processing. In other words, this is not a file manager that tries to be like Capture One, and that\'s how it should stay. Unlike many'

[07:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'apps I\'ve tested recently, this one doesn\'t use Electron, so it feels like a native app. It is written on a framework called Tauri, which is much better suited for this'

[07:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'task. This decision by the developers means that the application takes up little space— just under 400 megabytes under load—and it doesn\'t make the fans hum when not in use. I'

[07:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'imported my test memory card with photos, and the app performed quite well, importing and previewing at a rate of 2.2 photos per second from a fast'

[07:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'external SSD connected to my M2 MacBook Pro. If you\'re importing, say, 50,000 photos, it\'ll take about 6.5 hours, give or take. I was amazed by'

[08:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the capabilities of AI. The object search is quite primitive, with only eight hard-coded options, but they work quite well, and the percentage of false positives was low. Similarly, basic search'

[08:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: ', which also runs on local AI, is surprisingly good. It " slows down" on complex queries, but for an open source application, I think most amateur photographers will be happy with it. The first thing'

[08:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I noticed was that the app works very fast: thumbnails load instantly, and scrolling through even thousands of photos happens without any delays. Of course, there are'

[08:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'no perfect apps, and I encountered a few issues during testing. The biggest drawback is that the program does not read existing image metadata—'

[09:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'neither from EXIF ​​data nor from sidecar files. The first is much more surprising than the second. So, this means that your ratings, keywords , tags, and captions created in other'

[09:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'apps don\'t show up here at all. I did a little digging on the discussion forum on the GitHub page, and the developer mentions that existing tags will be available in future updates'

[09:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: ', but there was no indication of how long this would take or whether this would apply to other EXIF or sidecar metadata. Another issue I encountered was the rendering speed'

[09:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for the full-size preview in the app. Double-clicking on a previously unseen photo made me wait 3–5 seconds for Canon RAW files and as long as 20 seconds for my Fuji RAW files.'

[09:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Looking into the settings, I found that there are two preview modes : RAW rendering and embedded preview (which uses embedded JPEG), and RAW rendering is set by default.'

[10:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Switching to inline viewing made a huge difference: images displayed instantly or in about a second, depending on the file type. You may have noticed that'

[10:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'photo apps are popping up at an alarming rate these days, all thanks to the explosive growth of " vibe coding." I don\'t know if LAP is one of them, but there is no mention'

[10:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of Claude or ChatGPT on the GitHub page. No matter how sincere the developer\'s intentions, many such apps have poor interfaces, workflow or performance issues, and are'

[10:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'often abandoned after 3 months when the developer loses interest. Hopefully, LAP will prove to be an exception , and the developer will continue to improve upon the excellent'

[10:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'foundations already laid. In particular, I hope he puts working with existing image metadata at the top of his to-do list, because I know for a fact: most photographers won\'t even'

[11:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'consider an asset manager that can\'t read the keywords, captions, or ratings they\'ve spent years creating. It\'s a fast and thoughtfully designed'

[11:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'asset manager with a great basic feature set and no unnecessary ambitions. If you don\'t need to transfer metadata from another application, I recommend it without hesitation. For everyone else, I advise you'

[11:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to wait until the developer changes their approach and adds this absolutely necessary feature. This concludes our review of LAP. Asset manager with great potential. Is this'

